CERN, European Organization for Nuclear Research1211 Geneva 23SwitzerlandJ.D. Blakeblake@cernvax.cern.ch1991/2/14         Instructions for rebuilding gcc on an OS-9/68000 system         -------------------------------------------------------These notes assume that the  programmer  updating  gcc  on  an  OS-9/68000system  has  obtained a working copy of gcc, from a binary distribution orby cross compilation.  It is  not  worth  attempting  to  build  gcc  withMicroware's  compiler  cc.  (See Martin Husemann's posting of 1990/6/12 incomp.os.os9 for the gory details.)I shall assume that gcc is being built in the directory /H0/GCC and itssubdirectories.  The variable home_gcc in Makefile.osk should be modifiedif this is not so; the variable HOST_LIBDIR may also need to be modified.The source code of gcc is placed in  /H0/GCC,  in  /H0/GCC/CONFIG  and  in/H0/GCC/OSKSRC.  Before  transferring the source files from a UNIX system,run the following command on the UNIX system:     unix2osk *-* config/*-*to replace "-" in file names by "_".I shall assume that the run  time  library  gnulib.l  and  the  executablemodules  gcc,  cccp and cc1 of the current gcc are placed in the directory/H0/GCC/STAGE1, and that the updated version of gnulib.l and gcc is to  beplaced  in  /H0/GCC/STAGE2.  The process may be repeated, using STAGE2 gccto build  STAGE3.  The  executable  modules  of  STAGE3  will  usually  beidentical  to those of STAGE2, but may not be if the new gcc includes codegenerator changes.  In this case, use STAGE3 gcc to  build  STAGE4,  whoseexecutable modules should definitely be the same as those of STAGE3.Building move_if_change and really_new--------------------------------------Before gcc can be rebuilt, the programs move_if_change and really_new mustbe  compiled and placed in the current execution directory (which I assumeto be /H0/GCC/BIN).  The following OS-9/68000 commands do this:     chd /H0/GCC/OSKSRC     chx /H0/GCC/BIN     make move_if_change really_newBuilding gcc, cccp and cc1--------------------------The following OS-9/68000 commands use the STAGE1 version of  gnulib.l  andgcc to build a new version in STAGE2:     chd /H0/GCC     chx /H0/GCC/BIN     copy CONFIG/m68osk.md md -r     copy CONFIG/out_osk.c aux_output.c -r     copy CONFIG/tm_osk.h tm.h -r     copy CONFIG/tm_m68k.h tm_m68k.h -r     copy CONFIG/xm_osk.h config.h -r     make -b -f=Makefile.osk all R=STAGE1 S=STAGE2 >-STAGE2/log01       >>-STAGE2/log02 GCCED=? CCCPED=? CC1ED=? &The make command and its parameters should be placed  on  a  single  line.The  three  question  marks ("?") in the command should be replaced by theedition numbers to be assigned to the new executable modules gcc, cccp andcc1  respectively.  As the command takes a long time to execute, it is runin the background with its standard  output  and  error  output  piped  tofiles.The five copy commands ensure that the slave copies  of  the  five  headerfiles  in  the  main directory match the master copies in the subdirectoryCONFIG.Installation of gcc on an OS-9/68000 system-------------------------------------------See the comments at the end of the file Build-osk-native.Building an OS-9/68000 native gcc by cross compilation------------------------------------------------------See the files Build-osk-cross and  Build-osk-native.  The  procedure  usesgcc  on  the  host system to build a cross compiler gccos9.  Gccos9 buildsthe OS-9/68000 native gcc.  Gccos9 uses Microware's  Unibridge  for  crossassembly and linking.Debugging aids--------------The program cmpr.c  in  GCC/OSKSRC  compares  two  OS-9/68000  relocatableobject modules, ignoring the time stamp in the object module header.The UNIX command scripts ftpdiff,  ftpcmp  and  ftpcmpr  may  be  used  tocompare  modules generated by cross and native compilation.  Ftpdiff callsdiff and is for text  files;  ftpcmp  calls  cmp  and  is  for  executablemodules; ftpcmpr calls cmpr and is for object modules.I have observed a difference in two versions of c_parse.tab.r because of adifference  in  errno.h.  The  Unibridge version of errno.h did not defineERANGE; the OS-9/68000 version did.  Differences in object  modules  couldin  principle be caused by differences in the evaluation of floating pointnumbers; I have not observed any such differences  in  the  components  ofgcc.